-- Running Q1 on these rows produces the group (10,3)
-- Running Q2 on these rows also produces the group (10,3)

-- However, note that if an extra WHERE condition were mistakenly placed before grouping (thereby filtering rows prior to aggregation) the COUNT might change. For instance, consider the intended query:
--    SELECT DEPT.NAME, COUNT(*) FROM DEPT WHERE DEPT.NAME = 10 GROUP BY DEPT.NAME
-- which would yield a count of 3, whereas Q1 using HAVING does not prefilter and would aggregate over all rows before discarding non-10 groups. This demonstrates that the location of a filtering condition (WHERE vs HAVING vs outer WHERE on a derived table) is crucial and that Q1 and Q2 are not generally interchangeable.

-- Under standard SQL the following test data produces the same results for Q1 and Q2, illustrating that no counterexample exists:
