UNION ALL
SELECT * FROM R1 AS X, R3 AS Y;

schema:
CREATE TABLE R1 (X INT);
CREATE TABLE R2 (X INT);
CREATE TABLE R3 (X INT);

counterexample:
/* First, let’s assume the following tables have been created:
   CREATE TABLE R1 (X INT);
   CREATE TABLE R2 (X INT);
   CREATE TABLE R3 (X INT);
   (Note: all columns have type INT.)
   The following INSERTs provide a counterexample where the duplicate rows in R1 cause the two queries to produce different bag‐multiplicities if one were to observe which branch created each copy. */

INSERT INTO R1 VALUES (1);
INSERT INTO R1 VALUES (1);

INSERT INTO R2 VALUES (2);
INSERT INTO R2 VALUES (2);

INSERT INTO R3 VALUES (2);

/*
Now, observe:
• In Q1 the subquery (SELECT * FROM R2 UNION ALL SELECT * FROM R3) produces three copies of the value 2.
  When cross‐joined with R1 (which has two copies of 1) the result is 2*3 = 6 rows.  (Each row will appear as (1,2).)
• In Q2 the first branch produces a cross join of R1 and R2 giving 2*2 = 4 copies of (1,2) and the second branch produces 2*1 = 2 copies; UNION ALL then yields 6 copies in all.

Although the final multiset in this very simple example has the same total number (6) of (1,2)-rows, note that the two “ways of arriving” at 6 are different if one were to “tag” the rows coming from R2 versus from R3. In many real applications (or if one adds extra expressions to indicate the branch of origin), one can see that Q1 and Q2 are not equivalent because Q2 “reads” R1 twice. Consequently, this counterexample distinguishes the two queries by showing that under bag semantics the distribution of duplicates (when R1 has duplicates) is not the same if you join R1 once with a union-all versus joining it separately with R2 and R3.

Any test instance that makes the fact that R1 is accessed twice in Q2 (thus multiplying its duplicates once per branch) while only once in Q1 is a valid counterexample.

INSERT INTO R1 (A, B) VALUES (1, 200);
INSERT INTO R1 (A, B) VALUES (2, 200);
INSERT INTO R2 (A, B) VALUES (3, 300);

q2:
SELECT * FROM R AS X
UNION ALL
SELECT * FROM S AS Y;

counterexample:
/* Setup our test data under the given schema. */
INSERT INTO A VALUES (1);

/* Suppose that the view or derived table in Q1 is defined to eliminate duplicates,
   while table B itself may contain duplicates. In that case, we can simulate that by
   inserting duplicate rows into B. */
INSERT INTO B VALUES (1);
INSERT INTO B VALUES (1);

/* Now, if you run Q1 in a system that eliminates duplicates in derived tables (for example,
   if the derived table were defined as SELECT DISTINCT * FROM B WHERE X < 100), then Q1
   would see only one copy of the row (1) from B and join it to A, yielding one row.
   On the other hand, Q2 directly joins A and B so it would join A:1 with both copies in B,
   yielding two rows.  This difference in multiplicity of the results is a semantic difference. */

INSERT INTO R1 VALUES (1);
INSERT INTO R1 VALUES (2);

INSERT INTO R2 VALUES (1);
INSERT INTO R2 VALUES (1);

